<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hmannan Yazawin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Glass Palace Chronicle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hmannan Yazawin, or Glass Palace Chronicle, is the official chronicle of the Burmese monarchy, compiled by a committee of scholars appointed by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a> in 1829 and completed in 1832. Drawing on earlier chronicles and inscriptions, it narrates the history of the Burmese kings from legendary origins to 1821, and it remained the standard account of Burmese history into the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin.jpg" alt="Hmannan Yazawin" width="320" height="546" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glass_Palace_Chronicle.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally, the period produced the <b>Hmannan Yazawin</b> (Glass Palace Chronicle, 1832), legal compilations, court drama and poetry, and important Buddhist scholarship, including the Fifth Buddhist Council convened by Mindon in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s relative peace in the seventeenth century allowed a flowering of Theravada scholarship, monastic reform debates and Burmese-language literature, including legal texts and U Kala’s chronicle, on which later works such as the Konbaung <b>Hmannan Yazawin</b> drew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
